Fillets and chamfers: an analytic blend's contact that leaves its face at
a vertex V of the face's boundary ends on the edge of the face that holds
both V and the spine's end vertex, also where the hatching misses that
edge (mitcad#415).

The case: the recess's rim of 0691 and 0692 rounded up to the rib across
it; the rim's contact on the recess's cone leaves the cone exactly at the
vertex V where the rib's rounding meets its slope. Where the cone's edge
with the rib's rounding (from the spine's end vertex to V) has a curve
that stops short of V within V's tolerance, as an imported body's edge
may, the hatching of the contact on the cone found its end only on the
cone's other edge at V (with the slope). FillSD puts the end on an edge
holding the spine's end vertex when the hatching finds several edges at
the point; with only the slope's edge, the end was on it, and the corner
(PerformOneCorner, OnSame) took the face beyond that edge, the slope, for
the face at the end instead of the rib's rounding: the cut ran across the
bottom face and the result was invalid.

- FillSD (ChFi3d_Builder_SpKP.cxx): an end found at a vertex V of an arc
  that does not hold the spine's end vertex, where another arc of the face
  holds both V and that vertex: the end is on that arc, at V.

Before, the result was invalid. Test: the rib's edges with the cone
stopped 2e-5 short of their vertices with the slopes (geometry
test_fillet_results); the result is the exact body's.

